   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to employees classified as the Triborough bridge and tunnel authority superior officers benevolent association of an MTA-employer and the resolution of disputes in the course of collective negotiations",
    "basePrintNo": "S5815",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5815-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §209, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5815\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law, in relation to employees\nclassified as the Triborough bridge and tunnel authority superior\nofficers benevolent association of an MTA-employer and the resolution\nof disputes in the course of collective negotiations\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo allow certified or registered public employee organizations to\nrepresent employees as the Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority\nSuperior Officer Benevolent Association of an MTA-public employer and\nto be covered by the provisions of section 209 of the Civil Service\nLaw.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends section 209 of the Civil Service Law to grant\nbinding arbitration to Triborough bridge and tunnel authority superior\nofficers benevolent association members.\n\nSection two of this bill provides that this act shall take effect\nimmediately, provided, however, that the amendments to paragraph (f)\nof section 209(5) of the Civil Service Law made by section one of this\nbill shall not affect the expiration of such subdivision and shall be\ndeemed to expire therewith.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrent provisions of Chapter 929 of the Laws of 1986 relating to\ncompulsory arbitration to resolve impasses in collective negotiations\nbetween an MTA public employer and a public employee organization left\nout certain employees from receiving this benefit. This bill would\ncorrect this inequity.\n\nOver the years, Compulsory arbitration has allowed the people of New\nYork to enjoy uninterrupted services. All evaluations of the effects\nof this law have been positive and have advocated the continuation of\nthis process.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015: S.3524 - Vetoed mem .204\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided, however, that the\namendments to paragraph (f) of subdivision 5 of section 209 of the\nCivil Service Law made by section one of this act shall not affect the\nexpiration of such subdivision and shall be deemed to expire\ntherewith.\n",
